#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/id #uuid "1900f9ee-5295-5e56-b9d5-2dda8649b212" :question/math-academy-id "e-9684" :question/problem "The scatter plot above shows the distances some athletes in a sports club cover during a training session, and the time (in minutes) it takes. Use the trend line to estimate how long it takes a club athlete to run $10$ km.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/e5acbcf6efb94071c33cfaf698ee586e.png)" :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=10$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/1ad00f5afd6b7df9461f68043fd06474.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/3b7b5b068bbf97919b87f2563268cfd0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/9966735dc779084081b8242b65932697.png)\n\nTherefore, a club athlete can run $10$ kilometers in approximately $45$ minutes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc871c4-b557-5cff-8ab2-55d9cb6e24fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9690" :question/problem "The scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/c7145faa85aff583b7414d72979aed4c.png)\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3)$.*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/42a7269d76cd0018b398919003437c40.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) = (4,3)\n$$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 1.5}{4 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\\n&= 0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b$. We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + b \\\\\n3 &= 0.5(4) + b \\\\\n3 &= 2 + b \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n5 &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n0.5x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/id #uuid "3a864a65-e753-5bd5-b4fd-b3e95fc5a2de" :question/math-academy-id "e-9692" :question/problem "The scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/9ea29d6f32c24c53ff7138e53fa27e68.png)" :question/worked-solution "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 10x \\\\\n200 &= 10x \\\\\n\\frac{200}{10} &= x \\\\\n20 &= x \\\\\nx &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{mm}$.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83dad1b3-13a4-598d-b103-a011fc453713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177971} {:answer/id #uuid "01fbee12-0a98-5a04-a76d-8fff3ddefdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177972} {:answer/id #uuid "2556dbd8-0c0a-5469-8ba2-5b2917fa869d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177973} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbcef0f-5cad-5f1a-9861-ec095aa54bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177974} {:answer/id #uuid "412581cb-60af-50c7-ad2f-36832d96ae9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2556dbd8-0c0a-5469-8ba2-5b2917fa869d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177973} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a89c83-5249-5b02-913e-94468987dbad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177970}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04c26611-8a14-59f2-b00c-17923a404b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133772" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/q-133772.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020$.\nUse the trend line to estimate in which year the town's population was $56000$." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=56$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/a9d57651c5c342c3d619a20cfae2bc632df47f8924d49f400ed764870b7ae900.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/c816d47fc84f31c3a436818777d9473b68e9d8468051c40fe9b7b94005cc89ee.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e6cbfe34a6e3bd002171d2474542a12c66b7d88ce5989b0fbb4b3542f76a2155.png)\n\nTherefore, the population was $56\\,000$ in approximately the year $2000+10=2010.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95226290-f326-5889-a5ae-b16de6206a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177977} {:answer/id #uuid "b07fabd7-a9a9-56c7-b59b-2445ecd526e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177978} {:answer/id #uuid "3953a010-8a5a-56c6-a70f-6ca59b22bcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177979} {:answer/id #uuid "49c68931-8233-5c37-864f-ba9f6f7037eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177980} {:answer/id #uuid "6f41f20b-e695-569f-9228-1ef83dedfffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95226290-f326-5889-a5ae-b16de6206a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a2f426c-88d0-54c8-8e31-8a020a10250b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a2fa13-4227-5518-b473-4e39aa559784" :question/math-academy-id "q-133774" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/q-133774.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y =-0.5x + 9$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $x$ when $y = 2$." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We can estimate the required value by substituting $y=2$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2 & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2+0.5x & =9 \\\\ 0.5x & =7 \\\\ x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=2$ we have $x≈14.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=2$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb099db-d954-5129-8af4-e132998d3ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177983} {:answer/id #uuid "b896d53c-6262-5ca6-ad99-92aa32971cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177984} {:answer/id #uuid "13c0c627-d9c9-508d-b706-5fa6bd8fbab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177985} {:answer/id #uuid "574e6693-709a-5e87-bda7-147a26b6a64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177986} {:answer/id #uuid "67d58c2c-71c0-5b39-bc7d-f52edec4df01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574e6693-709a-5e87-bda7-147a26b6a64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177986} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5c2573-f603-5135-b60a-5ec09d9602f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177982}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b64eb719-1c3e-5ef0-9103-c543d4318cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-133779" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/q-133779.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town $(y)$ recorded $x$ years after $2000$.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y = 0.1x + 53$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the town's population in $2026$." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the year $2026$ is $26$ years since the year $2000.$\n\nTo predict the population in $2026,$ we need to substitute $x=26$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1x+53 \\\\  & =0.1(26)+53 \\\\  & =2.6+53 \\\\  & =55.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be approximately $55\\,600$ in $2026.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=26$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "966b8f4c-f637-591b-a829-5590327b4e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177995} {:answer/id #uuid "a332ac92-266c-5065-b0ea-4958a1717576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177996} {:answer/id #uuid "a93005a1-ac2f-5449-810d-77d93f36bb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177997} {:answer/id #uuid "10840ece-56ae-58bc-9907-10f21fd51c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177998} {:answer/id #uuid "3644e79b-4fc1-519c-976e-ba1eef3ec22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10840ece-56ae-58bc-9907-10f21fd51c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177998} :answer-field/id #uuid "653ac7ac-44a3-5b9e-9680-58825c2f8176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177994}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f98bb3a-554c-5cbd-87ea-206e88b640d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133785" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3753/Source/Images/q-133785.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020$.\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the year in which the population will be approximately $40000$.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2, 56)$ and $(18, 48)$.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/5b2ab039994e7c2306cad7e30717fb136b5dfc3a028db284a0e95f49b56d580f.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,56)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(18,48).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{48-56}{18-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{16} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,56):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 56 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 56 & =-1+b \\\\ 57 & =b \\\\ b & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+57.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=40$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 40 & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 0.5x+40 & =57 \\\\ 0.5x & =17 \\\\ x & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be $40\\,000$ around the year $2000+34=2034.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=40$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0454e4ed-7814-50dd-acdd-31d580709db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263215} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7346c0-dbae-5a98-966b-6f3f83a444d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263216} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c11544-7f53-53e1-9402-f897980b249e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263217} {:answer/id #uuid "1839868f-f495-58ec-b509-0cba03d0c0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263218} {:answer/id #uuid "6d08db34-747c-5d43-aa8c-8b372dec31ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1c11544-7f53-53e1-9402-f897980b249e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263217} :answer-field/id #uuid "509af303-d6d2-50fa-acaf-4cfc55a200f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263214}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79babf95-b234-5b7e-a39f-16410ad3c68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/4de50fc0b2b2f05b9ca60e28ffc65900a2b0780549fbf1cf32150a903a08faf5.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=20.$" :question/worked-solution "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=20$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/ad092e8773bbb8707dc95377b20b29a1f6ddab9e973b0a5fa469e15f47f967cc.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/520c396408909577337035a455f5078df148c491ec9d33a75a0f3bbdcaac9a5f.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/67b44784b133813b84204dc63aedf0a07b7edb67a30c52e3dc17d8567fcccb19.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=20,$ we obtain $x≈7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b9c4cc5-5880-5bbe-9366-370cc2de4c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263222} {:answer/id #uuid "0692e7e8-7b5e-5fa7-a7d5-480416fae2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263223} {:answer/id #uuid "319cbfe4-76a5-507f-a440-06c1c95d4f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263224} {:answer/id #uuid "c7193e4e-4c5f-590a-b1d5-67e2e5a89970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263225} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5a6372-6039-5898-99db-1694ca2037c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7193e4e-4c5f-590a-b1d5-67e2e5a89970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263225} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed52e1bf-733c-5819-aed7-8a62be46eed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8fd1d76-7104-56dc-931a-2f7d76cbe576" :question/math-academy-id "q-133884" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e00248d1dd5cb96a5e0099acf5da41913a805dbc388715f0b542aa821a95efa5.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the relationship between the number of hours $x$ a group of people spend watching TV each night and the time $y$ they sleep each night. The equation of the trend line is $y=10-1.25x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time a person sleeps per night if they watch TV for $6$ hours." :question/worked-solution "To predict the number of hours a person sleeps after watching TV for $6$ hours, we need to substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10-1.25x \\\\  & =10-1.25(6) \\\\  & =10-7.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a person watching TV for $6$ hours sleeps for approximately $2.5$ hours.\n\nNote: The point where $x=6$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcf59d67-61f0-5167-9bbe-e433b3537a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263229} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9db484-acab-5388-a06c-2f082001ce57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263230} {:answer/id #uuid "0a128075-51d8-547b-9672-aa3bdb33e0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263231} {:answer/id #uuid "493fe5fa-8041-5468-ab9a-a2c8b2e480ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263232} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b88070-c610-5498-a9ed-7d64dd99f04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf59d67-61f0-5167-9bbe-e433b3537a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd772e9-5f18-5103-82cc-ced26dc88acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263228}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f68282f1-36e0-5e8b-a38b-3f8d307a758e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133928" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/91e15117e7797cfce1b94c33d157c2e89fad519c80c715bfc3ef8082c1e38946.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a car $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of years $x$ since it was purchased. The equation of the trend line is $y=28-x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$" :question/worked-solution "To predict the number of years it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=20$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =28-x \\\\ 20 & =28-x \\\\ 20+x & =28 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it will take approximately $8$ years for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818c9164-83c7-551d-bcbc-6d1f39c9802a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263236} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd2b3a5-8e61-54e1-9c62-a5bbad0c5e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263237} {:answer/id #uuid "c5852581-47a7-5d89-a02c-c608c93d82bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263238} {:answer/id #uuid "eee37c69-621d-58d1-a6ef-235f80b2e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263239} {:answer/id #uuid "f175c626-c80f-5523-a491-d76b1472d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f175c626-c80f-5523-a491-d76b1472d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263240} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0bef1e-0621-57b8-b1f5-0963136b2494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263235}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87bad21a-36f4-5e06-9484-4b7826190ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-133929" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/ce3aec85d418c8f51988f819c04403f3f3ea2e0c0e35165ed729fc1e78d0f12f.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of decades $x$ since it was first created. The equation of the trend line is $y=3x+5.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the artwork to be worth $$32\\,000.$" :question/worked-solution "To predict the time that must pass before the artwork is worth $$32\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=32$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+5 \\\\ 32 & =3x+5 \\\\ 3x & =27 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $9$ decades for the artwork to have a value of $$32\\,000.$ \n\nNote: The point where $y=32$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e5166a-2778-5bb7-96ba-a8cbb31db659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263243} {:answer/id #uuid "6c12d04a-0dc5-54ed-b5ce-36e421066103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263244} {:answer/id #uuid "c113465b-8099-5725-8bd8-4d9b28180793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263245} {:answer/id #uuid "831bbf0d-a71d-520a-afc9-096bd17bf9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263246} {:answer/id #uuid "ac978aa9-c0b4-5e40-b2ce-dd4c0f8a527d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76e5166a-2778-5bb7-96ba-a8cbb31db659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263243} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e76129-8164-5989-a81c-797a36d5217d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263242}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89b89ff0-333a-5e1a-9504-209847c75169" :question/math-academy-id "q-133943" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/7ba7aa1cf49bb5a2dc430d9a21baaa8491aa61d8898f2aad142406c5c569b51b.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=7.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,4).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/59efe1b79b0d032179a5210ee694b0d59ad082853ca9fb80a9d79d9f6444f2bf.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.75$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75(1)+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75+b \\\\ b & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 7 & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 6.75 & =0.75x \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=7$ we have $x≈9.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cb7570-0dda-5dd7-bbca-b91721ccd562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263250} {:answer/id #uuid "765183a4-632f-5821-9fe0-f7cb462bac31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263251} {:answer/id #uuid "ea338e3a-14ec-55f2-ba91-eb3b9cb42a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263252} {:answer/id #uuid "e299eee5-ea23-58ba-b8e8-34ea4ee4540d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263253} {:answer/id #uuid "73cfaf92-4d30-5e72-b223-79e6c2631672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73cfaf92-4d30-5e72-b223-79e6c2631672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263254} :answer-field/id #uuid "89f65e2c-fa72-599a-879a-c4d8cd3e3175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e0da2a3-95bd-5475-bd22-74eac9a023c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133945" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/6de719fb9d16173e40759b507f16b0d10cd373059f5d127081d173bc0b3596b1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=0.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(21,9)$ and $(25,3).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/35797fe2923cdf9c741dda57a40401f4759cd722eb748f80d0df960c5d6eef29.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(21,9)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(25,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-9}{25-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(21,9):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+b \\\\ 9 & =-1.5(21)+b \\\\ 9 & =-31.5+b \\\\ b & =40.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+40.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 0 & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 1.5x & =40.5 \\\\ x & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=0$ we have $x≈27.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=0$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750aeaf6-de81-5309-80bf-cb41da17a102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "23674115-2ade-5706-a0fe-96b4831b77bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263258} {:answer/id #uuid "28283f1c-d248-50a2-9303-cc7600586f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263259} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b5f5aa-a75a-5419-acf1-a06330eb4022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263260} {:answer/id #uuid "6da48f8e-8e93-56a8-8417-e73e4079863f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da48f8e-8e93-56a8-8417-e73e4079863f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263261} :answer-field/id #uuid "36eedd8d-9014-536b-b934-27f97efb12f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263256}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8dce0e0-7da9-5218-b5ad-2cc27a39fb9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133960" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/001019dd20e7a56980201a07e94d3fb9e7824bdeef6864c90abb3172babce734.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the time (in minutes) it takes some professional cyclists to cover particular distances during training. \nUse the trend line to estimate how long it takes one of these cyclists to travel $4$ miles." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=4$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/19cf6fe36f3afb0322e51ab1d9b1d580453380ba67d813a923818756c1d57994.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/8815a1433c0c139ecf9370da8a6799b084a0e875a3bddb1b80ffa96792c9a145.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/f183ff82c15c750d47a0da10151917c02311260f0e86daecfeb1b12c9e15007c.png)\n\nTherefore, these cyclists travel $4$ miles in approximately $20$ minutes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78799658-f551-5ce6-b7f2-b741208e4045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263264} {:answer/id #uuid "e07c387c-e206-510c-8850-5b6b1b879689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263265} {:answer/id #uuid "4d80a4ce-eef0-5f24-865b-20991a0fd478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.5$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263266} {:answer/id #uuid "d918b1d8-5d42-58df-9b12-f88cf8a92b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263267} {:answer/id #uuid "c021ddcb-1e80-547e-aa39-dec005173694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c021ddcb-1e80-547e-aa39-dec005173694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263268} :answer-field/id #uuid "688dc202-b2a1-50fc-a159-751b75a9d7a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263263}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbb6ac4c-9e6a-53f5-af48-c57b58c5a491" :question/math-academy-id "q-133961" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/d3d0f871d60b7f7c8280abd7d3eba4337f910bae6b814586b368bbfb0a8dc313.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the weight loss per week (in pounds) vs. the number of hours spent training per week for a group of people. Use the trend line to estimate how much weight a person loses if they train for $4$ hours per week." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $x=4$ on the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/1e397473de815e2eda88ad33c59e2cf77c336b968ef6a1eeaffa67d9fbc64750.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/27c8ce8a7a1feadd4ea16d66f6a283fa245fa737036810924ead4b0148c6d6f5.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/a178070d1f62538a35e7e4e363a254c2afa776920079618b80b3bff358601871.png)\n\nTherefore, a person training $4$ hours a week will lose around $3$ pounds of weight."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2500233-0b03-5efa-94bd-4ddb139a7774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263271} {:answer/id #uuid "a48965f3-617b-5f94-8c62-d16be7f57480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,250$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263272} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6f7c83-d69b-570b-9412-565ba9b6dd05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263273} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a63350-f208-5143-a694-b30bfe0dc5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,250$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263274} {:answer/id #uuid "7c273700-1561-5633-9229-b381fb16844b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c273700-1561-5633-9229-b381fb16844b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263275} :answer-field/id #uuid "85615ef5-8e43-5bd3-aedd-a5b616822d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263270}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6261a08b-e81a-5e2c-ae24-27b897291587" :question/math-academy-id "q-133964" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/032c895f40d741c8468c531d96c763943a391cf7033ca4a63407d6b7b36bacef.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a motorcycle, in thousands of dollars, several years after it was purchased. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,5)$ and $(4,4).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/145a6a57bcd24c99e7de73f470de61c673b10f5a0d88b0c64527a17e52a0dc86.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+6 \\\\  & =-0.5(10)+6 \\\\  & =-5+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased is around $$1\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d50e0d-996d-5c55-9617-c703ad5ef198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263278} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ace2dd-509c-5955-ab1e-233583d1b47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263279} {:answer/id #uuid "670fee67-f4a1-5aad-9c60-7d7a5c0a7671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263280} {:answer/id #uuid "f4890c22-c1c1-5211-8a10-1363808020f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263281} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1d8849-9ee1-5632-9dcb-c46e9069bdb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1d8849-9ee1-5632-9dcb-c46e9069bdb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263282} :answer-field/id #uuid "3784e314-b53b-5dcc-b4be-2301a0181239" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263277}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58b0784b-138b-532b-836e-5e808016f755" :question/math-academy-id "q-133965" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e3319694b1112e92602219492d51116d514a88e885431ac1ef0c8f8becb0dc18.png)\n\nA tea shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature and the number of customers they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the number of customers the tea shop expects to serve if the outside temperature is ${36}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(15,60)$ and $(30,30).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/7adb5dd9bfcfcea8842eebfcacc704237f010f87c8ffd695afab890801237abd.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(15,60)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(30,30).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-60}{30-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-30)}{15} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(15,60):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 60 & =-2(15)+b \\\\ 60 & =-30+b \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+90.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=36$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+90 \\\\  & =-2(36)+90 \\\\  & =-72+90 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the tea shop expects to serve $18$ customers.\n\nNote: The point where $x=36$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336851ab-b199-5d2b-80a1-d3f3d93535f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263285} {:answer/id #uuid "24ab886a-44d2-56a1-af51-f7102d49fb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263286} {:answer/id #uuid "61c4344b-8f3b-5ba6-98ef-a3ef8f6954ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263287} {:answer/id #uuid "58d982f0-5a78-5596-afe3-eb3f45850519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263288} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6bf0b-281f-5737-93d9-916ef9128aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d982f0-5a78-5596-afe3-eb3f45850519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263288} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c3353f-76d0-5076-a4d2-cc000dc9c850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263284}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba0b6c74-cdaf-54be-b831-76776043acf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133967" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/1d4df2d201ccbaf2305534d61c8a0ed5f7957b1629d8cd2fbd5fff933283c0dc.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the number of matches won by some chess players in a championship and the hours they spend training per day.\nUse the trend line to estimate how much time players need to train each day to win $30$ championship games." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=30$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/26ba10258a8794a97dfc5e61c8160b342ed6890f7a9af40c1e21ac814c779188.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e50d79adb9803f6509314cf3512fcd388ccc78b287d6653b9e318ce54ce45022.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/a9bd5a7973ecb2e88c328d0943d4bfebe9862b2cea1abbd0b3fa3665fa177167.png)\n\nTherefore, a chess player that wins $30$ games spends around $5$ hours training per day."}]}
